@@ -0,0 +1,25 @@
+# Browser-machine file helper
+
+Use the matching daemon, extension and `bsk-file-host` from this branch. Local daemon mode keeps its existing local file paths. Server mode transfers bytes over the paired browser connection; it does not share the daemon filesystem with Chrome.
+
+## Install on the browser computer
+
+1. Build `cargo build --release -p bsk -p bsk-file-host` and `pnpm --filter @browser-skill/extension build`.
+2. Load the extension from `apps/extension/dist/chrome-mv3`, note its extension ID, and place `bsk-file-host` in its final program directory.
+3. Run `bsk-file-host install --extension-id <extension-id>`. It registers this executable with the current user's Google Chrome. No administrator access is needed. Re-run after moving the executable or changing the extension ID.
+4. Connect the extension using the daemon's server-mode pairing link. Existing `bsk upload` and `bsk download` commands then use the remote file transport.
+
+For Chromium, Chrome for Testing, or a nondefault profile on macOS/Linux, pass that browser's `NativeMessagingHosts` directory explicitly with `--manifest-directory`. Windows installation writes the Google Chrome user registry entry. Other Windows browsers require their own native-host registry entry. To remove the helper, remove its `com.browserskill.files.json` manifest (and the corresponding Windows native-host registry entry), then remove the executable.
+
+Registration writes only the Native Messaging host manifest and, on Windows, its Chrome registration. It does not install an extension, open browser tabs, or connect a daemon.
+
+## File lifecycle
+
+- IPC upload chunks first enter the daemon's existing session staging. The daemon forwards them to the extension's helper in 512 KiB chunks, then sends the browser-local path to the existing real file-input/drop implementation.
+- An upload means the control has received the file; webpage submission is a separate action. The helper retains uploaded files until session close, even after the invoking CLI releases its daemon copy.
+- Download capture keeps the existing Chrome download transaction. Before clicking, the extension connects to the helper. It imports only the completed file under this transfer's `BrowserSkill/tr_*` directory, then returns its actual size. The daemon reads the helper's chunks and supplies the existing `transfer.read` IPC result to the caller.
+- The helper copies a completed download into its private temporary directory and removes the browser download original. After byte transfer the daemon releases this helper copy. Session stop also releases uploads and unfinished transfers; closing the Native Messaging input releases remaining files.
+- Session IDs and transfer IDs are existing transport scope, not a new business identity or resource catalog. File bytes do not enter tool-result text. Upload/download responses remain filenames, metadata and transfer handles.
+- Helper errors end the current operation. No local/remote fallback or repeated page action is performed. The `native_files` flag is set by daemon mode, not by tool callers. `tool.file_transfer` is an extension-side internal operation, not a public arbitrary-path IPC method.
+
+The helper uses Chrome's [Native Messaging framing and host registration](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/extensions/develop/concepts/native-messaging). A 512 KiB byte chunk stays below the 1 MiB native-to-extension JSON message limit after base64 encoding.
